From 46f9d1a4768e2521095d089ab646debc3115181b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 10:16:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] www/i18n: translate the IPv6 protocol labels - the last RFW_
 residual

The IPv6 firewall protocol select on Reaper_Firewall.asp shipped its BOTH and
OTHER options as literal English. Done as one job per the backlog note, rather
than piecemeal.

- Minted RFW_257 ("Other") in all 25 packs, inserted after RFW_256 so ordering
  stays consistent. Lockstep held: every pack 6745 -> 6746 lines.
- BOTH now uses the existing RFW_252, which was already translated in all 25.
- TCP and UDP stay literal. They are protocol names, not words.

RFW_257 is fully translated rather than English-seeded, because RFW_252 is -
the register is matched to it, and the item is described as a translation
residual, so seeding English would have left it open.

Did NOT reuse Adaptive_Others ("Others"): it is plural and it names an Adaptive
QoS traffic category, not a protocol. That is the same class of mistake as
reusing ASUS's option_both_direction for "Both", which JP/CN/TW render as
"bidirectional".

The value="TCP|UDP|BOTH|OTHER" attributes are untouched and still uppercase.
rc/firewall.c:1520-1525 does an exact case-sensitive strcmp on them, so a
translated or re-cased value would break the rule format in 24 languages. Only
the labels changed; verified each value still appears exactly once.

Rule 29 checked: no RFW_257 translation contains a double quote (0/25).

Translations are mine, not a native pass. The gender-dependent ones are worth a
speaker's skim if one is available - CZ/PL/RU/UK/SL agree with a masculine
"protocol", DA/NO/SV/NL use the neutral list form. All are the generic dropdown
"Other" that router UIs conventionally use.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
